  "kind": "process",
  "title": "Written law codes",
  "summary": "Mesopotamia produced the earliest law codes: Urukagina's in the 24th century BCE, Ur-Nammu's around 2100–2050 BCE, and the 282 laws of Hammurabi (reigned 1792–1750 BCE), set on a public stele so that no one could claim ignorance. Rome's Twelve Tables of 451–450 BCE were displayed in bronze in the Forum. India's Laws of Manu were composed probably around the beginning of the Common Era. China's Tang Code, completed in 653, is the only early Chinese code surviving in full.",
